+# Final reflection
+
+## Purpose
+
+Look back honestly on the semester — this is the final, in place of a traditional exam (`docs/course-ethos.md`). Worth 10% of the course grade (`docs/grading-model.md`).
+
+## Do
+
+1. Pick **five** prompts from the bank below (or propose an instructor-approved equivalent) — at least one from each of the four categories.
+2. Answer each in your own words. These are pass/fail: any honest, specific answer earns full credit; vague or generic answers (“it was a good semester”) do not, but there is no single “correct” answer to chase.
+3. Share one favorite meme, quote, or moment from the semester — optional, but a nice way to close it out.
+4. If the section's Coding Odyssey Week 17 work includes the **Final Debrief** and Judgment Log review (`assignments/odyssey_gates/week-17.md`), that is this reflection's Odyssey-specific half — do not write two separate reflections. Use one of the five prompts for the rest of the semester's non-Odyssey material.
+
+## Prompt bank — pick 5, at least one per category
+
+**Growth in programming**
+
+- Describe the area where you grew the most as a programmer this semester, and what specifically helped you improve.
+- What can you do now with code that you could not do in Week 1?
+- What was the hardest concept to click — perhaps exception design, inheritance, recursion, or algorithmic efficiency — and what finally made it click?
+
+**A project or obstacle**
+
+- Describe the biggest obstacle you overcame and what kept you working at it.
+- Explain your favorite assignment and why it stuck with you.
+- Walk through one bug that taught you something real about how the computer (or you) actually think.
+
+**Learning habits and community**
+
+- How were you able to help a classmate this semester?
+- Describe your best tools or habits for getting support when you were stuck.
+- What would you tell a friend taking this class next semester that you wish someone had told you?
+
+**Next steps**
+
+- What steps did you take toward your career, and why did they matter to you?
+- Describe your plan for learning about job opportunities or companies that interest you.
+- Name one specific thing — a certification, project, or habit — you want to build on next semester, and why.
+
+## Support and integrity
+
+This is pass/fail and open-book/open-note/open-everything. Take the time needed, and revise and resubmit if you change your mind. If a prompt does not fit the semester, swap it for one that does; the categories matter more than exact wording.
